From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 6 Jul 2026 09:33:53 -0700
Subject: [PATCH net-next v1 00/15] net: flow_dissector: opt-in byte-identical fast paths + tunnel inner descent
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

__skb_flow_dissect() parses packet headers with a generic protocol-graph
parser. This series adds an opt-in, static-branch-gated, straight-line
fast path for the packet shapes that dominate real deployments. Each
shape has its own gate under /proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/, every gate is
disabled by default, and when a gate is off the added cost is one
not-taken branch per dissect.

The series is byte-identical by contract: for any packet, the fast path
either writes exactly the flow_keys bytes the slow path would have
written, or it returns false and the slow path runs. That contract is
enforced in-tree by a KUnit equivalence suite (patch 15).

Patch 1 stands slightly apart: it is a small win for the existing path --
today every dissect takes rcu_read_lock() and does two dependent
rcu_dereference() loads to look for a netns BPF flow dissector program
even when none is attached anywhere; a static key (mirroring
bpf_sk_lookup_enabled) skips all of it in the common no-program case.

Patches 2-8 are the byte-identical common shapes (eth+IPv4/IPv6 x
TCP/UDP, VLAN, QinQ, PPPoE, single MPLS, IP-in-IP, plain GRE). Patch 9
adds per-shape counters (/proc/net/flow_dissector_stats); patch 10 bounds
the fast-path tunnel recursion at MAX_FLOW_DISSECT_HDRS.

Tunnel inner-flow descent (patches 11-14)
-----------------------------------------
For VXLAN / Geneve / GTP-U / FOU / GUE the dissector stops at the outer
UDP header today, so every hash-based consumer (RSS, RPS, ECMP, bonding,
tc-flower, aRFS) sees the *outer* tunnel identity, not the inner flow.
All inner flows between a pair of tunnel endpoints collapse onto a single
hash bucket. GTP-U is the sharpest case -- no outer source-port entropy
at all, and it is the mobile-core data path where millions of subscriber
flows ride one tunnel between two endpoints.

Patches 11-14 add opt-in inner descent, each behind its own default-off
gate (net.flow_dissector.{vxlan,geneve,gtpu,gue,fou}_inner). Unlike an
earlier RFC posting of this idea, the descents are now byte-identical:
the slow path grows the same descent (__skb_flow_dissect_udp_encap()) as
the fast path (flow_dissect_fast_udp_inner()), so the fast and slow paths
never disagree. The only behaviour change is versus today's
non-descending default, and it happens only when an operator turns a gate
on -- a deliberate, per-tunnel-type choice, because it changes skb->hash
for that traffic. The KUnit suite covers the descents the same way it
covers the byte-identical shapes: with the descent gate pinned on it
dissects each tunnelled packet through the fast path and through the slow
path and memcmp()s the result (patch 15).

FOU and GUE (patch 14) are Tom Herbert's UDP encapsulations. Unlike
VXLAN/Geneve/GTP-U they have no well-known UDP port, so a packet cannot
be recognised from the wire alone; the dissector consults the per-netns
FOU table, reached from the built-in dissector through a small registered
ops hook (the fou module is tristate). GUE is self-describing; direct FOU
carries its inner protocol in that table rather than on the wire.

CPU for the descents is ~net-zero (the inner work cancels the outer
saving); the value is the inner-flow hash distribution/identity, and it
holds for IPv4 and IPv6 inner flows and underlays.

Performance summary
-------------------
Measured across 3 ISAs and 8 microarchitectures, with N=3 replicates
in-kernel and byte-identical verification per shape.

Reduction in dissector CPU cost (isolated per-shape microbench, all
fast-paths compiled in; range across the 7 measurable uarches):

      eth_ip    -4.7% .. -31.6%   (isolated eth+IPv4+TCP: -47% .. -55%)
      vlan     -18.1% .. -38.5%
      qinq     -16.1% .. -39.8%
      pppoe     -8.6% .. -28.0%
      mpls      -6.7% .. -15.0%
      ipip     -36.8% .. -47.0%   (largest; slow path re-parses inner IP)
      gre       byte-identical descent family (~ipip)

The tunnel inner descents (patches 11-14) are ~net-zero CPU; their value
is the flow distribution above, not a cycle saving.

Isolated eth + IPv4 + TCP, userspace A/B (same source compiled two
ways), nanoseconds per packet, lower is better:

    x86 Zen 2  (Threadripper PRO 3945WX)   12.4 -> 6.6   -47%
    x86 Skylake (Core i9-10885H)           10.6 -> 5.6   -47%
    x86 Haswell (Celeron 2955U)            35.9 -> 18.0  -50%
    ARM Cortex-A53                        119.0 -> 61.3  -48%
    ARM Cortex-A72                         42.4 -> 19.1  -55%
    ARM Cortex-A76                         19.4 -> 9.3   -52%
    RISC-V SpacemiT X60                    98.2 -> 49.3  -50%

The fast path executes about half as many instructions per dissect,
consistent across three ISAs / eight microarchitectures; the relative
reduction is largest on in-order cores. IPv6 uses the same gate and is
byte-identical (x86 -39%, RISC-V -25%).

flow_keys -> skb->hash is the canonical flow identity consumed by ~50
call sites: ECMP/multipath, RSS, RPS/RFS, bonding/LAG, tc-flower, aRFS.
Everything that spreads or classifies a flow derives from it, so the
per-dissect saving applies to all of them at once.

Relationship to the BPF flow dissector
--------------------------------------
The fast path is invoked strictly after the netns BPF flow dissector
hook in __skb_flow_dissect(): if a program is attached and returns any
verdict other than BPF_FLOW_DISSECTOR_CONTINUE, the function returns
before flow_dissect_fast() is reached. Attached BPF dissectors therefore
always take precedence, and a system running one sees bit-for-bit
unchanged behaviour from this series (patch 1 additionally makes its
program lookup cheaper for everyone else). Dissects fully handled by a
BPF program are deliberately not counted in
/proc/net/flow_dissector_stats (patch 9).

Maintenance burden: why two paths won't diverge
-----------------------------------------------
The slow path remains the single source of semantic truth and the fast
path is verified against it, not vice versa. Three layers enforce that:

  1. The eligibility gate: only the two standard dissectors and a flag
     subset ever enter flow_dissect_fast(); custom tc-flower dissector
     instances are structurally unaffected.
  2. Every shape is a default-off static branch: a kernel with gates off
     executes the not-taken-branch slow path only.
  3. The KUnit equivalence suite (patch 15) dissects a corpus of
     eligible shapes, tunnel descents, deliberate fast-path misses,
     truncations at every byte boundary, and skb-mode entry conditions
     through both paths and memcmp()s the resulting struct flow_keys.
     Future divergence is a failing test, not a silent behaviour change.

The test earns its place: during development it caught real divergences
the out-of-tree A/B harness had missed -- the MPLS fast path skipping the
slow path's out_good terminal writes, the 4in6 descent not reproducing
the outer-IPv6 residue, and (for the descents) the VXLAN/Geneve/GTP-U
fast path failing to set FLOW_DIS_ENCAPSULATION because the inner leaf
zeroed it. All fixed in this posting.

Robustness and safety limits
----------------------------
The fast path descends into IP-in-IP, GRE and (patches 11-14) UDP tunnels
by recursion. The slow path bounds header descents at
MAX_FLOW_DISSECT_HDRS; patch 10 gives the fast path the same bound, and
the UDP-tunnel descents route through a shared helper that carries it
too, so a crafted overlay-in-overlay chain falls back to the slow path at
the same depth rather than driving one C stack frame per ~20 bytes of
linear header. The KUnit suite covers the tunnel-bound boundary (a nested
VXLAN chain past the cap), the non-linear-skb fallback, and a seeded
fuzzer as a general guard.

A separate RFC thread proposes an adaptive auto-enable controller built
on the patch-9 counters (it manages only the byte-identical shape gates;
the descent gates are never auto-managed, since enabling them changes
hashing and must stay a deliberate operator action).

Testing: 3 ISAs / 8 uarches (x86 Zen1/Zen2/Skylake/Haswell, ARM
Cortex-A53/A72/A76, RISC-V X60), N=3 in-kernel replicates, in-tree KUnit
(patch 15) and out-of-tree A/B. Supporting data (flow-distribution study,
per-shape/per-arch matrix, break-even derivation):
https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2 , under perf-results/ .

